  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "For $n>1$ prove that\n$$1+\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14+\\cdots+\\frac1n$$\nis never an integer.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The sum is never an integer.\n\nWith the least common denominator, exactly one term has an odd numerator; the resulting numerator is odd and the denominator is even.",
      "worked": "Let $L=\\operatorname{lcm}(1,2,\\ldots,n)$, and let $2^k$ be the largest power of 2 not exceeding $n$. Since $n>1$, $k\\ge1$. The highest power of 2 dividing $L$ is exactly $2^k$.\n\nAmong $1,2,\\ldots,n$, only $2^k$ contains this full power of 2: its next odd multiple, $3\\times2^k$, exceeds $n$, since $n<2^{k+1}$. Therefore $\\frac{L}{2^k}$ is odd, while every other $\\frac{L}{j}$ is even.\n\nWriting the sum over the common denominator,\n$$1+\\frac12+\\cdots+\\frac1n=\\frac{L+L/2+\\cdots+L/n}{L},$$\nthe numerator is odd and $L$ is even. An even denominator cannot divide an odd numerator, so the sum is never an integer.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
